  "narrative": "I applied the registered signed-direction convention throughout. For direction -1 mean differences, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency in the exposed group because dependency scores are more negative for stronger dependency; for direction +1, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency in the exposed group. Because dependency scores are negative-valued and not centered at a stable zero, I continued to use an absolute dependency-score separation of roughly 0.1 as the practical scientific benchmark, with sign and interval precision decisive.  The new PTEN-STAT3 context probes are unusually coherent and large. H127, in TP53-intact cells, estimated +0.287 (95% CI +0.227 to +0.347; n=212 exposed, 1382 comparator), clearly supporting stronger STAT3 dependency with PTEN loss; I accept and close it. H128, in TP53-loss cells, estimated +0.190 (95% CI +0.085 to +0.296; n=54 exposed, 352 comparator), which is positive and above the point threshold but has only modest exposed-cell precision and a lower bound slightly below the 0.1 benchmark. I accept the direction as evidence for a dataset-specific TP53-loss stratum effect, but the exact magnitude is uncertain, so I keep H128 active and request independent validation as the one useful validation for this batch. H130, H147, H148, H149, and H150 further show PTEN-loss stronger STAT3 dependency in Avana library, PIK3CA-intact, PIK3CA-mutant, CDKN2A-intact, and SMARCA4-intact contexts. All estimates are positive, most intervals exclude 0.1, and they support robustness of the PTEN-STAT3 association within these measured contexts. I accept and close these as dataset-specific context checks. I do not interpret them as causal or externally generalizable; residual molecular co-occurrence, lineage subtype context, and multiplicity remain limits.  The specificity checks for STAT3 are clean. H151 tested CDKN2A loss against STAT3 and estimated only -0.005 (95% CI -0.032 to +0.021), rejecting a meaningful CDKN2A-to-STAT3 association. H152 tested SMARCA4 loss against STAT3 and estimated only -0.005 (95% CI -0.032 to +0.022), rejecting a meaningful SMARCA4-to-STAT3 association. Together with earlier PTEN-null results on MDM2 and USP7, this strengthens the interpretation that PTEN loss is relatively STAT3-specific among the axes tested, though it does not prove exclusivity against unmeasured predictors.  The MTAP baseline check H153 asked whether MTAP loss is associated with stronger USP7 dependency among TP53-intact cells. The estimate was -0.019 (95% CI -0.041 to +0.004), entirely below the practical benchmark and not supportive of a stronger-dependency effect. I reject it and close it. This argues against a simple explanation in which TP53-intact MTAP-loss cells alone have stronger USP7 dependency, but it does not resolve the still-imprecise TP53-loss/MTAP-loss attenuation question. The MTAP loose end therefore remains an unresolved exploratory issue, not a confirmed mechanism.  Cross-repair-network probes were uninformative in the direction registered. H154 tested KEAP1 loss against WRN and estimated +0.007 (95% CI -0.023 to +0.037), well below benchmark, so I reject a meaningful KEAP1-to-WRN association. H155 tested MSI-high against USP7 and estimated +0.057 (95% CI -0.013 to +0.127); the point estimate is below the practical benchmark, but the interval admits a modest positive effect, so I leave it unresolved and deferred rather than overclaiming absence of effect.  Overall, the new evidence strongly reinforces PTEN loss as a dataset-specific predictor of stronger STAT3 dependency across several measured strata, while supporting target specificity for the established axes. The only validation request is H128 because it is the newly accepted stratum with the least interval precision and the most direct scientific value in confirming the TP53-loss context of the PTEN-STAT3 axis.",
