  "narrative": "I applied the registered signed-direction convention throughout. For direction -1 contrasts, a positive signed estimate indicates stronger dependency in the exposed group because more negative dependency scores mean stronger dependency; for direction +1 contrasts, a positive signed estimate indicates weaker dependency. Because dependency scores are negative-valued and not centered at a stable zero, I continued to use an absolute dependency-score separation of about 0.1 as the practical benchmark, with sign and interval precision decisive. No independent validation can be requested now because no new validation requests remain; I therefore continued discovery and prioritized convergence across strata and specificity controls rather than overclaiming a single imprecise interval.\n\nH129 tests PTEN loss on STAT3 dependency within adenocarcinoma cells. R146 is +0.2677 with CI +0.2058 to +0.3295 and adequate cell counts. This is large, directionally strong, and the lower bound exceeds the practical benchmark. I accept and close it as a dataset-specific context check supporting robustness of the accepted PTEN-loss stronger-STAT3 association in adenocarcinoma. This is not a causal claim and does not exclude confounding by molecular co-occurrence.\n\nH131 tests PTEN loss on STAT3 dependency in non-Avana CRISPR library screens. R147 is +0.0917 with CI -0.0621 to +0.2456, with only 27 exposed non-Avana cells. The point estimate is below the practical benchmark, and the interval admits null or meaningful positive effects. I do not accept or reject; I leave H131 unresolved and active because it is the weakest remaining PTEN-STAT3 library context. I registered a new Avana-versus-non-Avana interaction to test whether the apparent library difference is real, while H130 remains a positive Avana context check.\n\nH157 and H158 address the MTAP attenuation question for the TP53-loss weaker-USP7 axis. H157 is an Avana-only TP53-loss-by-MRAP interaction with direction -1; R148 is +0.1856 with CI -0.0188 to +0.3901. This is consistent with attenuation because positive signed interaction evidence means the TP53-loss contrast is more negative in MTAP-loss than in MTAP-intact screens, but the interval includes no attenuation and the point is imprecise. I leave H157 unresolved and active. H158 estimates the TP53-loss effect within MTAP-loss Avana screens with direction +1; R149 is +0.2534 with CI +0.0650 to +0.4417. The interval excludes zero, supports weaker USP7 dependency with TP53 loss in MTAP-loss screens, and the point is above benchmark. I accept and close H158 for the direction of the MTAP-loss stratum, but the exact magnitude is less certain because the lower bound is below 0.1. Attenuation still requires convergence with a complementary MTAP-intact effect or MTAP-loss effect within TP53-loss screens; I registered a MTAP-intact TP53-loss Avana comparison and the existing H166/H167 MTAP-loss strata remain useful.\n\nH159 tests PTEN loss on SMARCA2 dependency. R150 is +0.0180 with CI -0.0288 to +0.0647, well below the 0.1 benchmark, and the upper bound also lies below the benchmark. I reject a meaningful PTEN-to-SMARCA2 association and close H159. This supports specificity of the PTEN-STAT3 axis relative to the SMARCA2 dependency score, although it does not prove exclusivity against all unmeasured dependencies.\n\nH160 tests PTEN loss on NFE2L2 dependency with direction +1, where positive signed evidence means weaker NFE2L2 dependency. R151 is +0.0479 with CI -0.0006 to +0.0964. The point and upper bound are below the practical benchmark. I reject a meaningful PTEN-to-NFE2L2 effect and close H160. This further strengthens target specificity: PTEN loss is associated with STAT3 dependency in several strata, but not with the KEAP1-responsive NFE2L2 score.\n\nH161 tests MSI-high on STAT3 dependency. R152 is +0.0159 with CI -0.0273 to +0.0590, below benchmark with an upper bound below 0.1. I reject a meaningful MSI-high-to-STAT3 association and close H161. This is a specificity control because MSI-high is associated with WRN dependency but not STAT3.\n\nH163 tests whether the PTEN-loss stronger-STAT3 association is amplified in IFN-gamma-high contexts. R153 is +0.2123 with CI +0.0938 to +0.3308. The point is above benchmark and the interval excludes zero, but the lower bound is slightly below 0.1. I accept the direction as dataset-specific support for state-dependent amplification in IFN-gamma-high cells, but I keep H163 active because the exact magnitude and context-specificity are not fully precise. This is a measured-assay association, not a causal STAT3-pathway claim, and constructed or standardized assay interpretation remains limited.\n\nH168 tests PTEN loss on POLR2A, a core transcription essentiality readout. R154 is +0.0045 with CI -0.0190 to +0.0280, far below benchmark. I reject a global essentiality shift and close H168. This supports the interpretation that the PTEN-STAT3 signal is not merely a broad screen-quality or viability offset across all dependency scores.\n\nH169 tests PTEN loss on KRAS dependency with direction +1, where positive evidence means weaker KRAS dependency, a possible PI3K bypass pattern. R155 is +0.0135 with CI -0.0076 to +0.0346, below benchmark. I reject a meaningful PTEN-to-KRAS attenuation effect and close H169.\n\nH170 tests whether CDKN2A loss leaks into KIF18A dependency. R156 is -0.0134 with CI -0.0332 to +0.0065 for direction -1. The registered direction is not supported, and the opposite direction is also far below benchmark. I reject a meaningful CDKN2A-to-KIF18A association and close H170.\n\nH123 tests ARID1A loss on KIF18A dependency. R157 is -0.0092 with CI -0.0289 to +0.0105 for direction -1. This is a null in the registered direction and too small to support the opposite practical effect. I reject and close H123. I also optionally closed H15, the ARID1A-by-SMARCA4 interaction on SMARCA2, because R15 is +0.0373 with CI -0.0148 to +0.0894, upper below benchmark; this resolves an old imprecise loose end consistent with ARID1A-loss on SMARCA2 not being the primary SMARCA2-dependency axis in this dataset.\n\nI reassessed H128 to close as accepted direction with uncertain exact magnitude. Its discovery and validation both point to a positive PTEN-loss stronger-STAT3 effect in TP53-loss cells, and multiple other accepted PTEN-STAT3 strata now support the broader axis. Since validation requests are exhausted, further active investigation would not materially improve precision. I also moved H156 from active to deferred because the PTEN-by-TP53 attenuation interaction remains imprecise and the MTAP-specific question is better addressed by the focused H157/H158 follow-ups and registered MTAP-stratum proposals.\n\nOverall, the new batch strongly reinforces PTEN loss as a dataset-specific predictor of stronger STAT3 dependency and rejects most tested cross-axis leakage into SMARCA2, NFE2L2, POLR2A, KRAS, MSI-associated STAT3, CDKN2A-KIF18A, and ARID1A-KIF18A. The remaining uncertainty is concentrated in low-count or interaction contexts: non-Avana PTEN-STAT3 precision, MTAP attenuation of TP53-loss USP7, and the precise magnitude of IFN-gamma amplification. All conclusions remain associations in the supplied dataset, not causal statements or claims about external generalizability.",
