lity, PIK3CA status, CDKN2A and SMARCA4 contexts, library, Cas9, IFN-gamma, EMT, stemness, hypoxia, proliferation, and serum contexts. These axes are large relative to the comparator and far above the 0.1 practical absolute benchmark, and the main axes received independent validation. Context-specific acceptances should not be counted as separate replications because they overlap with the broad validated effects; their value is showing that the effects are not obviously confined to a small subgroup. Negative and specificity findings are also important. Driver-gene hypotheses were not supported: KRAS mutation did not indicate stronger KRAS dependency H1; EGFR amplification did not indicate stronger EGFR dependency H3 with null validation; ERBB2 amplification did not indicate stronger ERBB2 dependency H4; BRAF V600E did not indicate stronger BRAF dependency H5; and many related subgroup, co-mutation, and interaction refinements were null. MYC amplification was null for the tested core outputs POLR2A, DHX9, RPL11, MDM2, and KIF18A. TMED10 was generally null across tested predictors, and many POLR2A comparisons were null. PTEN loss was not a global dependency shift: it was not meaningfully associated with MDM2 H137, USP7 H138, SMARCA2 H159, NFE2L2 H160, or POLR2A H168, supporting specificity for STAT3 among the tested readouts. ARID1A loss alone was not associated with SMARCA2 dependency and did not appear necessary for the SMARCA4-loss axis. The main unresolved scientific issue is MTAP attenuation of the TP53-USP7 axis. TP53-loss-associated weaker USP7 dependency appears smaller among MTAP-loss cells than MTAP-intact cells, with the direct TP53 effect in MTAP-loss cells H67 about +0.284 compared with about +0.421 in MTAP-intact cells H68, but the interaction H18 is imprecise and the direct MTAP-loss versus MTAP-intact effect within TP53-loss is directionally positive yet underpowered: H216 +0.119 with interval -0.075 to +0.312, and the lower-Cas9 stratum H217 +0.144 with interval -0.146 to +0.434. This is consistent with MTAP loss partially offsetting TP53-loss-related USP7 hypo-dependency, but it does not establish a scientifically meaningful modifier effect. I closed PTEN-loss versus PTEN-intact STAT3 probes by high versus low aneuploidy, H212 and H213, because the PTEN-STAT3 association was validated overall and is already supported across many independent-context direct estimates; testing additional overlapping strata adds little compared with resolving the MTAP question. The most informative next analysis is a focused direct estimate of MTAP-loss versus MTAP-intact USP7 dependency among TP53-loss cells, H216 and any carefully pre-specified technical strata, with the following interpretation: a positive effect with lower bound above the practical benchmark would support MTAP loss as an offsetting modifier of TP53-loss-associated USP7 hypo-dependency, whereas a null or small precise effect would leave the attenuation pattern as a subgroup-generated hypothesis. All conclusions remain associations in the supplied dataset; causality, pathway mechanism, clinical actionability, and external generalizability remain unestablished because of possible co-mutation confounding, incomplete Cas9 and growth-rate data, library and batch effects, multiple testing, and subgroup selection.",
