  "narrative": "I assessed all newly delivered discovery results using the signed convention and practical acceptance criteria: valid cell sizes, a 95% Welch interval excluding zero, and a scientifically meaningful dependency-score separation, interpreted in light of the stated 10% clinical-significance threshold. Because dependency scores are negative-valued and arbitrary in units, I treated absolute magnitudes around 0.1 or larger, especially when supported by large standard deviations, as potentially meaningful. I did not request validation because no new independent-validation requests remain.\n\nH240 and H241 directly clarify the research-signature-A specificity concern. In the signature-A-high stratum, the TP53-loss-associated weaker USP7 dependency claim is not supported: R230 is +0.003 with CI -0.110 to +0.116, cells 207/765. The point estimate is essentially null and the interval excludes large or moderate effects above roughly 0.12, though it cannot exclude a small positive effect; I leave H240 unresolved but active because the new follow-up proposals examine whether MTAP-loss status explains this stratum-specific attenuation. In the signature-A-low stratum, R231 is +0.805 with CI +0.777 to +0.833, cells 199/829; H241 is accepted as a context-specific supplied-dataset association. This strongly suggests that the TP53-loss-USP7 axis is not uniform across the constructed signature-A strata, but the biological interpretation of research signature A is undefined and should not be interpreted as biology.\n\nH242 and H243 directly test the PTEN-loss-associated stronger STAT3 dependency in marker-E-positive and marker-E-negative strata. Both are supported: R232 is +0.328 with CI +0.255 to +0.401, and R233 is +0.204 with CI +0.130 to +0.278. The association persists in both marker-E strata, with a somewhat larger effect in marker-E-positive cells. These are robustness acceptances within the supplied dataset, not independent replications, and the constructed marker itself has no assigned biology.\n\nH244 and H245 remain central unresolved questions for MTAP-loss attenuation of the TP53-loss-USP7 axis under higher-quality screen conditions. R234 is +0.238 with CI -0.045 to +0.521, cells 36/149/164/552; R235 is +0.220 with CI -0.061 to +0.502, cells 36/164. Both point in the attenuation direction under the signed convention but have intervals including zero and modest cell sizes in the MTAP-loss cells. I therefore keep them unresolved and active. The broadened MTAP-loss and research-signature-A-by-MTAP follow-up proposals are the most informative next tests before over-interpreting this attenuation.\n\nH246-H249 test screen-quality robustness. PTEN-loss associated stronger STAT3 dependency is supported in both high-quality and lower-quality screen ROC-AUC strata: R236 +0.253 CI +0.181 to +0.325, and R237 +0.280 CI +0.205 to +0.355. TP53-loss associated weaker USP7 dependency is also supported in both screen-quality strata: R238 +0.426 CI +0.332 to +0.519, and R239 +0.360 CI +0.251 to +0.469. These acceptances reduce concern that screen ROC quality is a confounder for these two major axes.\n\nH250 and H251 test whether two very large accepted axes persist in arbitrary marker-E-positive cells. R240 is +0.790 CI +0.762 to +0.817 for SMARCA4-loss associated stronger SMARCA2 dependency, and R241 is +0.796 CI +0.767 to +0.825 for KEAP1-loss associated weaker NFE2L2 dependency. Both are accepted. This supports robustness of the major axes, while the earlier marker-E interaction remains a constructed-assay specificity concern rather than a biological claim.\n\nOverall, the new evidence strengthens the robustness of PTEN-loss STAT3, TP53-loss USP7, SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2, and KEAP1-loss NFE2L2 associations across screen-quality and marker-E strata. It also sharpens two unresolved audit issues: whether research signature A reveals hidden constructed-assay structure or a real but biologically undefined modifier, and whether MTAP-loss attenuation of TP53-loss USP7 is real but underpowered. All conclusions remain associations in the supplied dataset only; none support causal, mechanistic, clinical, or external generalizability claims."
