  "narrative": "No assessments were due and no new independent validation results arrived; validation request capacity is exhausted, so no claim was newly validated or rejected at this synthesis step. I reassessed three previously valid direct context tests of the accepted CDKN2A-loss/MDM2-dependency axis (H78, H79, H82) because their estimates were clinically meaningful and their 95% intervals excluded zero; they are now accepted as context-specific supplied-dataset associations. The major supported axes remain stable. MSI-high cells show markedly stronger WRN dependency, about +0.79 to +0.80 signed to the accepted stronger-dependency direction, with independent validation and robustness across Cas9 activity, Avana-library status, and aneuploidy strata. KEAP1-loss cells show markedly weaker NFE2L2 dependency, about +0.80, with independent validation and robustness across Cas9, library, aneuploidy, and arbitrary-marker strata. SMARCA4-loss cells show markedly stronger SMARCA2 dependency, about +0.78 to +0.79, with independent validation; ARID1A loss alone does not produce this effect. TP53-loss cells show weaker USP7 dependency, about +0.40 to +0.45, validated and robust across screen quality, Cas9 activity, library, lineage, and MTAP-intact strata. CDKN2A-loss cells show stronger MDM2 dependency, about +0.12 overall, with replication in TP53-intact, TP53-loss, non-Avana, and direct MTAP-stratum tests. PTEN-loss cells show stronger STAT3 dependency, about +0.27, validated and robust across TP53 status, library, arbitrary marker, screen quality, serum, hypoxia, growth rate, and driver-negative contexts; this is reinforced by null PTEN effects on several other dependencies. Negative findings are important: driver-associated dependencies were largely not supported. KRAS mutation was not associated with stronger KRAS or RIT1 dependency; EGFR amplification was not associated with EGFR dependency and its validation was null; ERBB2 amplification was not associated with ERBB2 or RIT1 dependency; BRAF V600E was not associated with BRAF dependency. MYC amplification was broadly null across tested dependencies, TMED10 was null across tested predictors, and MTAP loss was not a simple main-effect driver for USP7 or MDM2. Interactions involving arbitrary research signatures/markers are not accepted as biology: signature A shows extreme stratum heterogeneity for TP53-loss USP7, and marker E showed an unexpected opposite interaction with PTEN-loss STAT3 despite robust direct strata. The principal unresolved issue is MTAP-loss attenuation of the TP53-loss USP7 association. Direct and interaction probes often point in the attenuation direction, with positive signed attenuation estimates around +0.12 to +0.24, but key intervals include zero and cell sizes are modest; validation of the MTAP-loss TP53 subgroup was imprecise. Therefore attenuation remains plausible but unresolved. Screen-quality and Cas9-quality tests reduce the concern that the accepted major axes are technical artifacts. The most informative next analysis is an adequately powered pre-specified test of the TP53-loss by MTAP-loss interaction on USP7 dependency, ideally in independent data. Within the supplied dataset, the newly registered Cas9-low and high-quality Avana direct MTAP comparisons, together with pending signature-A/MTAP strata, are the best next tests. If MTAP attenuation persists with intervals excluding zero, it may be accepted as a context-specific association; if not, it should be treated as underpowered or null. Arbitrary-signature anomalies should be interpreted as assay-specificity concerns unless they persist across independent strata and quality contexts. All conclusions remain associations in this supplied dataset, not causal, mechanistic, clinical, or externally generalizable claims.",
