  "narrative": "I assessed all newly delivered discovery results using the signed convention: each estimate and interval is already oriented to the registered hypothesis direction. Acceptance required valid cells, an interval excluding zero, and a dependency-score separation large enough to be scientifically meaningful on this scale. Because these are numerous subgroup and interaction probes in a supplied dataset, I treated positive subgroup results as context-specific associations only, not causal or external claims.\n\nThe research-signature-A/USP7 anomaly was further clarified. In signature-A-high cells, TP53 loss was not supported as being associated with weaker USP7 dependency. H253 (signature-A-high, MTAP-intact) gave a small positive signed effect +0.054 with CI -0.068 to +0.177; the interval includes zero and cannot exclude a small-to-modest positive effect, so it is unresolved but closed because further probing of this exact null stratum is not informative. H254 (signature-A-high, MTAP-loss) gave a negative signed effect -0.265 with upper bound only +0.030; this argues strongly against the registered positive effect and is rejected. H260 (signature-A-high, Cas9-high) gave -0.049 with CI -0.231 to +0.132, so it is unresolved but closed; it provides no support. By contrast, H255 (signature-A-low, MTAP-loss) gave +0.736 with CI +0.671 to +0.801 and is accepted as a context-specific association. Importantly, H267 and H269 show that the accepted CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 and MSI-high/WRN axes remain strong in signature-A-high cells: +0.137 with CI +0.085 to +0.189, and +0.807 with CI +0.756 to +0.858, respectively. This reduces concern that research signature A is a global hidden confounder and confines the signature-A anomaly mainly to the TP53-loss/USP7 axis or to constructed-assay heterogeneity.\n\nMTAP-specific attenuation of the TP53-loss/USP7 association remains unresolved. H261 directly shows that TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency in MTAP-loss/CDKN2A-intact cells: +0.284 with CI +0.086 to +0.482, accepted. This does not itself prove attenuation; it only shows the effect remains present. The corresponding interaction H262 points toward attenuation under the signed direction (+0.132), but CI -0.080 to +0.343 includes zero and the MTAP-loss/CDKN2A-intact cell sizes are modest. H264 tests CDKN2A-loss alone and is essentially null: +0.004 with CI -0.170 to +0.177; unresolved/closed. H263 is invalid due to insufficient cell counts. Thus attenuation is plausible but not supported as a separate accepted claim.\n\nCas9-activity-low strata were also informative. TP53 loss is associated with weaker USP7 dependency in Cas9-low/MTAP-intact cells, H257: +0.440 with CI +0.325 to +0.556, accepted. It is also associated with weaker USP7 in Cas9-low/MTAP-loss cells, H256: +0.295 with CI +0.025 to +0.564, accepted, though the point estimate is smaller and exposed cells are only 33. This pattern is compatible with attenuation but is not a direct interaction test. Overall, the TP53-loss/USP7 association remains robust across several strata, while MTAP-specific attenuation remains the central unresolved issue and H262 is kept active. No validation is requested because no new independent-validation requests remain. All accepted conclusions remain supplied-dataset associations and do not establish causality, mechanism, clinical relevance, or external generalizability.",
