, growth rate, hypoxia, screen quality and arbitrary-marker strata, with PTEN effects on other dependencies largely null. Important negative findings are equally central. Canonical driver-allele-to-dependency mappings were not supported in this dataset: KRAS mutation was not associated with stronger KRAS or RIT1 dependency (H1 and H2); EGFR amplification was not associated with stronger EGFR dependency and independent validation was null (H3; R3 and R13); ERBB2 amplification was not associated with ERBB2, RIT1 or TMED10 dependency (H4, H104 and H33); and BRAF V600E was not associated with BRAF dependency (H5). MYC amplification was broadly null across tested dependencies, TMED10 was null across tested predictors, MTAP loss was not a simple main-effect driver of USP7 or MDM2 dependency, and media serum percentage was not associated with STAT3 dependency. The principal unresolved issue is whether MTAP loss attenuates the TP53-loss-associated weaker USP7 dependency. Direct subgroup effects are smaller in MTAP-loss than MTAP-intact cells, and several interaction or quality-restricted probes point in the attenuation direction, but intervals commonly include zero, exposed cells are modest in important strata, and validation of the MTAP-loss TP53-loss contrast was imprecise. Accordingly, MTAP attenuation remains plausible but not accepted. Research signature A showed a striking anomaly for TP53-loss USP7 dependency, but CDKN2A-loss MDM2 and MSI-high WRN effects remained strong in signature-A-high strata (H267 and H269), making the anomaly more consistent with constructed-assay heterogeneity than a global technical confounder. Arbitrary research markers and signatures should not be interpreted as biological mechanisms. The most informative next analysis is an adequately powered, pre-specified external test of the TP53-loss by MTAP-loss interaction on USP7 dependency, with enough co-altered cells to estimate an interaction of roughly 0.20; secondary useful work is external replication of the stable PTEN-loss STAT3 and TP53-loss USP7 axes. Additional post hoc searches over constructed signatures or markers are unlikely to improve inference unless pre-specified and adequately powered. All conclusions remain associations within the supplied dataset and do not establish causality, biological mechanism, clinical relevance, or external generalizability.",
