"narrative": "No new independent validation estimates were delivered in this synthesis window and no assessments_due were listed; the validation request budget is exhausted. The final assessment therefore relies on the supplied discovery ledger plus prior validation estimates. All statements are associations in this supplied 2000-cell, lung adenocarcinoma-dominant CRISPR dependency dataset; they do not establish causality, mechanism, clinical relevance, or external generalizability. Because dependency scores are continuous and more negative means stronger dependency, I judged meaningfulness by absolute separation on the observed dependency-score scale, interval precision, replication across strata, and prior independent validation rather than by a direct 10% relative rule. Signed estimates are already oriented to the registered hypothesis: a positive signed estimate with direction -1 means raw exposed-minus-comparator is negative, i.e. stronger dependency; a positive signed estimate with direction +1 means raw exposed-minus-comparator is positive, which for dependency means weaker dependency. Supported robust findings. 1. MSI-high cells have markedly stronger WRN dependency. This is the strongest validated axis: signed +0.790 in discovery R10 and +0.799 in validation R39. It remains large across Cas9-activity strata, Avana and non-Avana libraries, aneuploidy strata, lineage contexts, mutation-burden-adjacent contexts, matched RNA+DNA restriction, research-signature strata, and MTAP strata. The effect is far larger than the typical WRN standard deviation, so it is scientifically meaningful even though external generalizability is unproven. 2. KEAP1-loss cells have markedly weaker NFE2L2 dependency. The accepted reverse-direction claim H13 is supported by signed +0.796 discovery R6 and +0.799 validation R38. The original stronger-NFE2L2 hypothesis is rejected. This association is large and robust across library, Cas9, aneuploidy, lineage, matched-omics, and arbitrary-marker contexts. 3. SMARCA4-loss cells have markedly stronger SMARCA2 dependency. Signed +0.784 discovery R19 and +0.790 validation R65 support H20. This holds in ARID1A-loss and ARID1A-intact cells, whereas ARID1A loss alone is not accepted as producing the SMARCA2 effect. 4. TP53-loss cells have weaker USP7 dependency. Signed +0.396 discovery R11 and +0.448 validation R52 support H11. This axis is robust across screen-quality, Cas9, library, aneuploidy, lineage, mutation-burden, matched-omics, MTAP-intact, CDKN2A-intact, and combined clean contexts; the clean TP53-loss effect in MTAP-intact and CDKN2A-intact cells is still large, signed +0.423 in R256. 5. CDKN2A-loss cells have stronger MDM2 dependency. The main accepted effect is signed +0.117 discovery R93. Smaller but repeatedly supported: TP53-intact signed +0.119 discovery R59 with validation +0.129 R78; TP53-loss signed +0.111 discovery R81 with validation +0.132 R92; non-Avana signed +0.125 discovery R95 with validation +0.172 R105. A 9p21-low interaction attenuation claim was not supported, so the MDM2 effect is not reduced to a simple copy-number co-deletion artifact. 6. PTEN-loss cells have stronger STAT3 dependency. Signed +0.267 discovery R113 and +0.283 validation R119 support H116. This is unusually robust across TP53 status, driver-negative cells, serum conditions, growth-rate and hypoxia strata, screen quality, arbitrary marker strata, and MTAP strata. PTEN effects on several other dependencies, including WRN H282, are null, strengthening specificity within this dataset. Important negative findings. Canonical driver-allele-to-self-dependency mappings were not supported: KRAS mutation was not associated with stronger KRAS dependency H1 or KRAS in adenocarcinoma H16; EGFR amplification was not associated with stronger EGFR dependency H3, with validation null R13, and was not supported in EGFR/adenocarcinoma or KRAS-WT contexts; ERBB2 amplification was not associated with stronger ERBB2 dependency H4 or related dependencies; BRAF V600E was not associated with stronger BRAF dependency H5. MYC amplification was broadly null across tested POLR2A, DHX9, RPL11, KIF18A, MDM2, and WRN probes. TMED10 dependency was largely unassociated with tested molecular predictors. Media serum percentage was not associated with STAT3 dependency. MTAP loss was not accepted as a simple main-effect driver of USP7 or MDM2 dependency. Many secondary interactions, including BRCA2 loss with WRN or DHX9, STK11 loss with KRAS, RB1 loss with KIF18A, PIK3CA interactions, and many lineage/library/Cas9 interactions, were null, too small, or unresolved. These negative results matter: the dataset does not support a simple canonical genotype-to-self-dependency model for the driver loci tested, even though several non-self dependency associations are strong and validated. Principal unresolved uncertainty. MTAP loss may attenuate the accepted TP53-loss-associated weaker USP7 dependency, but this is not yet a supported independent claim. The accepted main TP53-loss USP7 effect is large and robust, while MTAP-loss strata and interaction estimates are generally smaller and imprecise. The available attenuation-direction evidence is suggestive: H67 signed +0.284 discovery R57 with validation +0.265 R106 but validation interval crossing zero; H146 signed +0.137 R17 crossing zero; H273 CDKN2A-intact interaction +0.139 R254 crossing zero; H283 direct high-Cas9 CDKN2A-intact contrast +0.123 R266 crossing zero and underpowered; H274 Avana CDKN2A-intact MTAP-loss contrast +0.180 R255 crossing zero. These are compatible with attenuation but do not establish it. The registered direct broader probe H295 remains the most informative supplied-dataset follow-up: MTAP-loss effect on USP7 within TP53-loss, CDKN2A-intact cells. It should be accepted only if the signed interval excludes zero with a meaningful magnitude, not merely because the point estimate points toward attenuation. A second unresolved/interpretive issue is research-signature A heterogeneity for USP7: H241 shows a very large TP53-loss USP7 effect in signature-A-low cells, while H240 is near null in signature-A-high cells, and the interaction is striking. However, core axes such as MSI-high WRN and CDKN2A-loss MDM2 remain strong within signature-A strata. I therefore treat this as constructed-assay heterogeneity, not as evidence of a global technical confounder or assigned biology for research signature A. Arbitrary research markers D, E, and F similarly should not be assigned biological mechanism even where accepted associations remain stable within their strata. Other claims remain unresolved because they were not tested, had insufficient cells, or have intervals too wide to decide practical importance. Final conclusion. The evidence supports six stable dataset-internal dependency axes: MSI-high with stronger WRN, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2, SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2, TP53 loss with weaker USP7, CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2, and PTEN loss with stronger STAT3. It does not support canonical driver-allele self-dependency associations for KRAS, EGFR, ERBB2, or BRAF in this dataset. It does not establish that MTAP loss attenuates TP53-loss USP7 dependency, although this remains the highest-priority unresolved interaction. The most informative next analysis is a pre-specified, adequately powered external replication of the TP53-loss by MTAP-loss interaction on USP7 dependency, ideally designed to estimate an interaction near 0.20 dependency-score units. If only supplied-dataset follow-up is possible, analyzing H295 is the best internal next step. Secondary external replication targets are PTEN-loss STAT3 and TP53-loss USP7, plus confirmation of the core MSI-high WRN, KEAP1-loss NFE2L2, SMARCA4-loss SMARCA2, and CDKN2A-loss MDM2 axes. Additional post hoc mining of constructed research signatures or arbitrary markers is low priority.",
"research_notes": "Final synthesis iteration 22. No new validation delivered and validation request budget exhausted. No assessments_due. Maintain no new registered claims in final report; retain existing H295 active as the most informative next internal analysis if the controller later permits analysis: direct MTAP-loss contrast within TP53-loss, CDKN2A-intact cells to test USP7 attenuation with more power than the high-Cas9-only H283 probe. Accepted scientific core axes are: MSI-high stronger WRN; KEAP1-loss weaker NFE2L2; SMARCA4-loss stronger SMARCA2; TP53-loss weaker USP7; CDKN2A-loss stronger MDM2; PTEN-loss stronger STAT3. Major negatives are lack of driver self-dependency mappings for KRAS/EGFR/ERBB2/BRAF and broad nulls for MYC amplification, TMED10, media serum, BRCA2, STK11, RB1, and many interaction probes. Unresolved: MTAP-loss attenuation of TP53-loss/USP7 is plausible but imprecise; signature-A USP7 heterogeneity likely constructed-assay heterogeneity; several active/no-evidence claims remain unresolved. External generalizability, causality, and mechanism remain unestablished."
}

