  "narrative": "All twelve newly delivered comparisons had valid Welch cells and were assessed using the already-signed estimates, with positive signed values interpreted in the registered hypothesis direction rather than recoding the raw contrast again. The four screen-quality strata sets, H296-H303, are best viewed as pre-specified robustness checks of already-supported axes rather than as claims that screen_roc_auc is a causal modifier or as independent external validation. They are nevertheless informative because both the high- and low-screen_roc_auc strata showed substantial associations in the registered direction: KEAP1 loss remained associated with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, signed +0.793 in high-quality screens, R282, and +0.798 in low-quality screens, R283; SMARCA4 loss remained associated with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, signed +0.791, R284, and +0.778, R285; CDKN2A loss remained associated with stronger MDM2 dependency, signed +0.132, R286, and +0.103, R287; MSI-high remained associated with stronger WRN dependency, signed +0.790 in both screen-quality halves, R288 and R289. These intervals exclude zero and cover magnitudes that are large relative to the relevant dependency-score scales. I therefore accept these stratum-specific associations as evidence that the core KEAP1/NFE2L2, SMARCA4/SMARCA2, CDKN2A/MDM2, and MSI-high/WRN associations are not confined to either screen-quality stratum. This does not eliminate all possible confounding because screen-quality metrics can themselves correlate with biological or technical features, but it argues against the simplest explanation that these main axes are artifacts of poor screen quality. H272 tests whether the KEAP1-loss association with weaker NFE2L2 dependency persists among PTEN-loss cells. The signed estimate is +0.776 with a tight interval, R281, so I accept it. This is conditional-association robustness: it shows the KEAP1/NFE2L2 axis remains present within PTEN-loss cells, but it does not estimate or support effect modification by PTEN loss because no interaction contrast was delivered. H304 tests whether MTAP loss modifies the PTEN-loss/STAT3 association. The signed interaction estimate is small, +0.022, and the interval includes both attenuation-like and enhancement-like effects, R279. It does not support a registered positive interaction claim. Given that this is one of many interaction probes and the evidence is not directionally consistent or statistically supported, I reject H304 as unsupported. I would not interpret the interval as proving the absence of any moderate modification; rather, the supplied data do not warrant accepting a separate MTAP-dependent PTEN/STAT3 claim. H305 tests CDKN2A loss as a predictor of WRN dependency after excluding MSI-high cells. The signed estimate is +0.005 with a narrow interval centered near zero, R280. This is a clear specificity null on the dependency-score scale and supports the conclusion that CDKN2A loss is not broadly associated with stronger WRN dependency in MSI-negative cells, reinforcing the more specific CDKN2A-loss/MDM2 association rather than a generic WRN association. H295 is the most important unresolved probe: MTAP loss among TP53-loss, CDKN2A-intact cells with respect to USP7 dependency. The signed estimate is +0.115, R278, which points in the attenuation direction expected if MTAP loss partially restores USP7 dependency in TP53-loss cells. However, the 95% interval is -0.100 to +0.329 and crosses zero. The exposed cell contains 61 observations and the comparator 264, so the test is not sufficiently powered to distinguish attenuation from no effect or even a modest opposite effect. The broader interval upper bound is compatible with a meaningful attenuation, while the lower bound is compatible with no meaningful attenuation or a small opposite association. Therefore H295 remains unresolved. I closed its investigation because no independent validation capacity remains and further supplied-dataset mining would add multiplicity without solving the precision problem. The attenuation hypothesis should not be promoted to an accepted claim. Overall, these results further support the six stable supplied-dataset axes: MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; TP53 loss with weaker USP7 dependency; CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 dependency; and PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency. They do not establish MTAP loss as a broad modifier, do not establish MTAP-loss attenuation of TP53-loss USP7 dependency, and do not restore canonical driver-allele self-dependency mappings for KRAS, EGFR, ERBB2, or BRAF. All conclusions remain associations in this supplied dataset, subject to residual confounding and multiplicity, and do not demonstrate causality, mechanism, clinical relevance, or external generalizability.",
  "research_notes": "Final supplied-dataset appraisal. No validation request was made because the new-request budget is exhausted. New screen-quality strata accept four core robustness claims: KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 in both high- and low-screen_roc_auc strata; SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 in both; CDKN2A loss with stronger MDM2 in both; MSI-high with stronger WRN in both. PTEN-loss cells also retain a large KEAP1-loss/weaker-NFE2L2 association. CDKN2A loss is not associated with WRN in MSI-negative cells, supporting specificity of CDKN2A/MDM2 over WRN. MTAP-loss attenuation of TP53-loss USP7 remains unresolved: H295 signed estimate +0.115 favors attenuation but CI crosses zero and is imprecise. MTAP-loss PTEN/STAT3 interaction is not supported and was rejected as unsupported. Stable accepted axes remain MSI-high/WRN, KEAP1-loss/NFE2L2, SMARCA4-loss/SMARCA2, TP53-loss/USP7, CDKN2A-loss/MDM2, and PTEN-loss/STAT3. Driver-self dependencies for KRAS, EGFR, ERBB2, and BRAF remain unsupported. All findings are dataset-internal associations, not causal or generalizable claims."
